{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1379 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "5e8d128e1394ef303c22b3affed50e6d33baa8238a5872c97781b5b52b1d19d3" :edb/db-before-t 1378 :edb/db-after-t 1379 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534691 50 #inst "2026-10-12T08:01:06.191Z" 13194139534691 17592186045417 true] [17592186166575 1071 "![](images/73/73b3d7951ab2c2a355763f71d2dd97dc4c9c5921bc087ae83af3936b0daf5d8a.png)\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" 13194139534691 17592186045417 false] [17592186166575 1071 "![](images/e3/e34a1c2d8bdb840fb249000a5d13a87a5d8253c63e84e5478e8f3b50abb4fa35.svg)\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ converges when $x=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$" 13194139534691 17592186045417 true] [17592186166576 1071 "![](images/1d/1da7fe97b22b7a72b4253abfb180ce5f294fa945450879d8cb857ee1470edbf3.png)\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{4}{\\pi(2n-1)} \\sin((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nUse this Fourier series to find the sum of the series\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}.\n$$" 13194139534691 17592186045417 false] [17592186166576 1071 "![](images/52/52dd34274df338ede8449a4a66a101b3c54089a15c3b4f25d21ef1884a5af6ec.svg)\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{4}{\\pi(2n-1)} \\sin((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nUse this Fourier series to find the sum of the series\n\n$$\n\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}.\n$$" 13194139534691 17592186045417 true] [17592186166960 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432955 13194139534691 17592186045417 true] [17592186166960 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432956 13194139534691 17592186045417 true] [17592186166960 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432958 13194139534691 17592186045417 true] [17592186166961 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432954 13194139534691 17592186045417 true] [17592186166961 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432957 13194139534691 17592186045417 true] [17592186166961 1038 #edb/ref 17592186432959 13194139534691 17592186045417 true] [17592186166993 1050 600.0 13194139534691 17592186045417 true] [17592186167294 1071 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}0, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{1}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi (2n-1)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.\n\nAt $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" 13194139534691 17592186045417 true] [17592186167294 1071 "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ 1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. The Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{1}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2}{\\pi (2n-1)}\\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)x).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below.At $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡((2n-1)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f(x)$ is \n{{field-2}}\nat $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf(\\frac{\\pi }{2})={{field-3}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{2n-1}={{field-4}}.\n$$" 13194139534691 17592186045417 false] [17592186168046 1064 "A powerful application of Fourier series is *evaluating infinite sums*. The strategy is to find a known Fourier series that contains the sum we want, and then choose a convenient value of $x$ to plug in.\n\nFor example, consider the function $F(x)=x^2$ defined on $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi).$ The Fourier series of the $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{\\pi^2}{3} + 4\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^2} \\cos(nx).\n$$\n\nLet's use this to find the value of the infinite sum $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^2}.$ \n\nNow, notice that at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(n\\cdot0\\right) = \\cos\\left(0\\right) = 1.\n$$\n\nBy the *pointwise convergence theorem*, since $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf\\left(0\\right) = 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, we have the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}(n\\cdot 0) \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot 1 \\\\ -\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3} & =4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{12} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^2} = -\\dfrac{\\pi^2}{12}.$" 13194139534691 17592186045417 false] [17592186168046 1064 "A powerful application of Fourier series is *evaluating infinite sums*. The strategy is to find a known Fourier series that contains the sum we want, and then choose a convenient value of $x$ to plug in.\n\nFor example, consider the function $F(x)=x^2$ defined on $x \\in [-\\pi,\\pi).$ The Fourier series of the $2\\pi$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x) \\sim \\dfrac{\\pi^2}{3} + 4\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^2} \\cos(nx).\n$$\n\nLet's use this to find the value of the infinite sum $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^2}.$ \n\nNow, notice that at $x=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos\\left(n\\cdot0\\right) = \\cos\\left(0\\right) = 1.\n$$\n\nBy the *pointwise convergence theorem*, since $f(x)$ is continuous at $x=0,$ the corresponding Fourier series converges to \n\n$$\nf\\left(0\\right) = 0.\n$$\n\n \n\nHence, we have the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(0) & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\cdot 0) \\\\ 0 & =\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3}+4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\cdot 1 \\\\ -\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{3} & =4{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}} \\\\ -\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{12} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\displaystyle\\sum_{n=1}^\\infty \\dfrac{(-1)^n}{n^2} = -\\dfrac{\\pi^2}{12}.$" 13194139534691 17592186045417 true] [17592186316573 1075 #edb/ref 17592186316160 13194139534691 17592186045417 false] [17592186316573 1078 #edb/ref 17592186315566 13194139534691 17592186045417 true] [17592186316573 1078 #edb/ref 17592186316160 13194139534691 17592186045417 false] [17592186316824 1075 #edb/ref 17592186315736 13194139534691 17592186045417 false] [17592186316824 1078 #edb/ref 17592186315133 13194139534691 17592186045417 true] [17592186316824 1078 #edb/ref 17592186315736 13194139534691 17592186045417 false] [17592186317396 1071 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{3},0] \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & x\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" 13194139534691 17592186045417 true] [17592186317396 1071 "$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{3},0] \\\\ 2\\operatornam